4. CONTRIBUTIONS

"Contribution" means any work you deliberately submit for inclusion in the
Software -- a pull request, a patch, or anything else offered for the same
purpose. Reporting a bug, writing about the Software, or creating Content
under clause 3 is not a Contribution, and nothing in this clause or in
CLA.md reaches any of them.

Contributions are governed by the OpenDoctrines Contributor License
Agreement, in CLA.md, which forms part of this license. Submitting a
Contribution means accepting it. In summary, and CLA.md governs if this
summary and it ever differ:

  (a) you assign the copyright in your Contribution to the Licensor, who
      becomes its owner and may use, change, relicense, sell or sublicense
      it without asking you, paying you or crediting you;
  (b) where the law does not permit that assignment, you grant instead the
      broadest exclusive license it does permit, to the same effect;
  (c) you waive your moral rights in the Contribution as far as the law
      allows, and agree not to assert what cannot be waived;
  (d) you keep a perpetual license to use your own Contribution anywhere
      else, for any purpose, including commercially; and
  (e) you confirm the work is yours to give, and that no employer,
      university or other license conflicts with that.

Why this clause exists, stated plainly: clause 5 lets the Licensor relicense
the Software, but that power reaches only rights the Licensor holds. A
contributor who keeps the copyright in an accepted patch keeps a veto over
what the Software as a whole can later become, and the patch cannot be
removed years later without rewriting it. Assignment settles that at the
moment the work arrives rather than leaving it to be discovered. The license
back in (d) is the counterweight: what a contributor gives up is exclusivity,
not the use of their own work.


